tors":["Burgos, A"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":"Topic: This introductory survey course on the social construction of race in the United States examines how various conceptions and interpretations of racial difference have changed over time in U.S. history and the ways these differences meshed with social inequalities. Specifically, we explore the ways the color line had a profound impact on everyday life. Through readings, lectures, discussion, and writing assignments, we will engage the construction of race through the realm of the law, culture, and immigration policy. We will interrogate how race was made real (and continues to be) in immigration policy, determining who was/is permitted to enter the United States as an immigrant at various historical moments, whether they were permitted to naturalize as “free white persons,” and their treatment once in U.S. society. Indeed, although it is socially constructed, race remains a very real lived experience then as now."}]},{"id":"LLS 282","subject":"LLS","number":"282","title":"Feminist and Queer Activisms","creditHours":"3 hours","minHours":3,"genEd":["1US"],"degreeAttributes":"Cultural Studies - US Minority course.","scheduleNote":"","info":"Same as AAS 282 and GWS 282. See GWS 282.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"81228","code":"TCB","typeCode":"LCD","group":"lecture","status":"unknown","statusText":"","partOfTerm":"1","dateRange":"","meetings":[{"typeName":"Lecture-Discussion","typeCode":"LCD","days":["T","R"],"start":570,"end":650,"location":"106B1 Engineering Hall","instructors":["Beauchamp, T"]}],"restrictions":[],"coRequest":null,"specialApproval":null,"sectionText":null}]},{"id":"LLS 308","subject":"LLS","number":"308","title":"Spanish in the United States","creditHours":"3 hours","minHours":3,"genEd":[],"degreeAttributes":"","scheduleNote":"","info":"Same as SPAN 308. See SPAN 308.","linkedByLetter":false,"sections":[{"crn":"69392","code":"E","typeCode